Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action gothic. Candlelight, deep shadows, desaturated palette. NARRATOR: lean man of middle years, pale skin, dark disordered hair, sharp restless eyes, plain dark coat and waistcoat. THE OLD MAN: frail elderly man, thin white hair, one pale blue vulture eye filmed over, nightshirt. Officers in dark period coats and badges.

FADE IN:

INT. BARE CHAMBER - NIGHT

A single candle burns on a plain wooden table. NARRATOR sits facing us in a hard chair, lean and pale, dark hair disordered, eyes too bright. His hands rest too still on his knees.

(SOUND: candle flame soft hiss, distant floorboard creak)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

He leans a fraction closer. The candle gutters.

(SOUND: faint quick tick, like a watch under cloth)

NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Shutters half-drawn. THE OLD MAN sits by the window in a worn chair, frail, thin white hair, nightshirt under a shawl. His pale blue vulture eye—filmed, dull—turns toward the doorway.

(SOUND: soft morning birds far off, chair wood creak)

NARRATOR stands in the doorway, coat neat, smiling with forced warmth. He does not look long at the eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. BARE CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR in the chair again, a thin smile. He lifts one finger as if instructing a child.

(SOUND: candle spit, house settling)

NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. The room black as pitch; shutters close fastened. The door latch turns with exquisite slowness. A dark lantern, fully closed, slides into the gap. NARRATOR's head follows—inch by inch—until he can see THE OLD MAN asleep on the bed, eye shut.

(SOUND: latch whisper, hinges almost silent, NARRATOR's careful breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! And then, when I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head. I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening.

His fingers undo the lantern a hair's breadth. A single thin ray seeks the pillow—finds the eye closed.

(SOUND: tin hinge creak, soft and hated)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this? And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Morning light. NARRATOR strides in boldly, hearty, calling out. THE OLD MAN sits up in bed, shawl about his shoulders, the filmed eye half-lidded.

(SOUND: shutters thrown, floorboards under firm steps)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

THE OLD MAN
Well enough.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. The door opens even more slowly. NARRATOR's face in the crack—triumph barely held. THE OLD MAN lies still in the thick dark.

(SOUND: watch-tick silence, latch slower than a minute hand)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

A chuckle escapes him—soft, involuntary. On the bed, THE OLD MAN starts and sits up.

(SOUND: bedclothes sudden rustle)

THE OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR freezes in the opening, lantern still shut, not a muscle moving. THE OLD MAN remains upright, listening into the pitch black.

(SOUND: death-watch beetles faint in the wall, NARRATOR's held breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight groan—low, stifled, from the bottom of the soul.

(SOUND: that groan; wind fretting the chimney)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. I knew the sound well. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. He had been saying to himself—It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor—or It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp. All in vain; because Death, in approaching him had stalked with his black shadow before him.

INT. BARE CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR's face close in the candlelight, eyes wide, almost gentle.

(SOUND: candle hiss, his swallow)

NARRATOR
And it was the mournful influence of the unperceived shadow that caused him to feel—although he neither saw nor heard—to feel the presence of my head within the room.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Still the dark. NARRATOR's thumb works the tin fastening—stealthily. A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots out and falls full upon the vulture eye.

(SOUND: tin slip, then thick silence)

The eye is open—wide, wide open. Dull blue, hideous veil. Nothing else of the face is seen; only that damned spot in the ray.

(MUSIC: low tense drone beginning under the quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

Then—under everything—a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat, soft at first)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless. He scarcely breathes. The ray stays on the eye. The heartbeat quickens—louder, louder.

(SOUND: heartbeat rising, quicker, a drum under cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. But even yet I refrained and kept still. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant. The old man's terror must have been extreme!

Sweat on NARRATOR's brow. His free hand trembles once, then stills.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder still; a board ticks in the heatless room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror. Yet, for some minutes longer I refrained and stood still. But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell he throws the lantern wide and leaps into the room. THE OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: yell; single shriek; bed frame crash; muffled struggle)

NARRATOR stands over the wreckage, smiling gaily. Under the bed—still—the muffled heartbeat.

(SOUND: muffled heart, many beats; NARRATOR's quick breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This, however, did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall.

The sound thins... falters... ceases. NARRATOR heaves the bed aside. He kneels, places his hand on the old man's heart, and holds it there.

(SOUND: silence after the heart; cloth shift as he presses)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. I removed the bed and examined the corpse. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. BARE CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR in the chair, almost proud, hands open as if to show them clean.

(SOUND: candle, a soft satisfied exhale)

NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Lantern light. NARRATOR works in silence and haste: the corpse dismembered—head, arms, legs. A tub stands ready; no stain reaches the floor. He lifts three planks from the chamber flooring, deposits the remains between the scantlings, replaces the boards with cunning care. He runs his palm over the seams—flush, innocent.

(SOUND: saw short and wet; planks pry and settle; tub water lap; no drip on wood)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs. I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

INT. HALL AND STREET DOOR - NIGHT

Still dark as midnight. A bell sounds four. Then—knocking at the street door. NARRATOR descends with a light heart and a candle, pale face composed to welcome.

(SOUND: distant bell four times; firm knocks on wood; NARRATOR's easy steps)

He opens. Three men on the threshold in dark coats and badges: OFFICER REYNOLDS (graying, calm), OFFICER HALE (younger, watchful), OFFICER CLEMM (broad, polite).

(SOUND: door hinges, night air)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play has been aroused. We are to search the premises.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen—welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Candlelight. NARRATOR leads the three officers through. He shows the old man's treasures—secure, undisturbed. In the enthusiasm of his confidence he brings chairs and sets them out. Then, wild audacity in his perfect triumph, he places his own seat upon the very spot beneath which the corpse reposes.

(SOUND: chairs dragged, floor solid under their boots)

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues. You see—all is as it should be.

The officers sit. OFFICER HALE glances about; OFFICER CLEMM nods, satisfied. They chat of familiar things. NARRATOR answers cheerily—then a shade paler. He touches his temple.

(SOUND: pleasant low talk; then a faint ringing, almost imagined)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease. But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The sound clarifies: low, dull, quick—such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. NARRATOR's eyes flick to the floorboards under his chair. The officers hear nothing; they smile on.

(SOUND: heartbeat under cotton, steady, growing; officers' calm murmurs)

NARRATOR talks more freely, more fluently, voice heightening. He gasps once for breath. He rises, argues about trifles in a high key, violent little gesticulations. The noise steadily increases.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder; chair legs scrape)

NARRATOR
(vehement, too loud)
—and as I say, the weather, the hour, trifles—nothing—

He paces the floor with heavy strides. He foams, raves, swears under breath. He seizes the chair he sat on and grates it upon the boards—yet the noise rises over all.

(SOUND: heavy pacing; chair wood grating; heartbeat louder—louder—louder)

(MUSIC: dissonant pulse locked to the heart)

OFFICER REYNOLDS and the others continue to chat pleasantly, smiling. NARRATOR stares at their mouths, their calm eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? Oh God! what could I do? Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!—this I thought, and this I think. But anything was better than this agony! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!

He stops over the spot. The smile of the officers burns him. He shrieks—

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He jabs at the floor. The officers spring up. The heartbeat pounds once more—then the candle shakes in the draft of their movement.

(SOUND: his shriek; boots sudden on boards; heart under the planks like a drum)

INT. BARE CHAMBER - NIGHT

Back to the chair. NARRATOR sits as at the first, spent, eyes still too bright. The candle is lower. He looks at us a long moment—neither smile nor plea.

(SOUND: candle near its end; house utterly still—no heart)

NARRATOR
How, then, am I mad?

He does not move. The flame pinches out.

(SOUND: wick die; dark)

FADE OUT.

THE END
